{"key":"ics/T0830","id":"T0830","name":"Adversary-in-the-Middle","domain":"ics","level":"technique","parent_id":null,"tactics":["collection"],"platforms":["None"],"environments":["ics-ot"],"environment_basis":"project_taxonomy_from_upstream_platforms","summary":"Adversaries with privileged network access may seek to modify network traffic in real time using adversary-in-the-middle (AiTM) attacks. This type of attack allows the adversary to intercept traffic to and/or from a particular device on the network. If a AiTM attack is established, then the adversary has the ability to block, log, modify, or inject traffic into the communication stream. There are several ways to accomplish this attack, but some…","source_url":"https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T0830","source_stix_id":"attack-pattern--9a505987-ab05-4f46-a9a6-6441442eec3b","source_modified":"2025-04-16T21:26:16.777Z","source_object_version":"2.0","telemetry":{"status":"mapped","components":[{"id":"DC0038","name":"Application Log Content","url":"https://attack.mitre.org/datacomponents/DC0038","stix_id":"x-mitre-data-component--9c2fa0ae-7abc-485a-97f6-699e3b6cf9fa","page":"telemetry/DC0038/"},{"id":"DC0085","name":"Network Traffic Content","url":"https://attack.mitre.org/datacomponents/DC0085","stix_id":"x-mitre-data-component--3772e279-27d6-477a-9fe3-c6beb363594c","page":"telemetry/DC0085/"},{"id":"DC0078","name":"Network Traffic Flow","url":"https://attack.mitre.org/datacomponents/DC0078","stix_id":"x-mitre-data-component--a7f22107-02e5-4982-9067-6625d4a1765a","page":"telemetry/DC0078/"},{"id":"DC0032","name":"Process Creation","url":"https://attack.mitre.org/datacomponents/DC0032","stix_id":"x-mitre-data-component--3d20385b-24ef-40e1-9f56-f39750379077","page":"telemetry/DC0032/"},{"id":"DC0060","name":"Service Creation","url":"https://attack.mitre.org/datacomponents/DC0060","stix_id":"x-mitre-data-component--5297a638-1382-4f0c-8472-0d21830bf705","page":"telemetry/DC0060/"},{"id":"DC0063","name":"Windows Registry Key Modification","url":"https://attack.mitre.org/datacomponents/DC0063","stix_id":"x-mitre-data-component--da85d358-741a-410d-9433-20d6269a6170","page":"telemetry/DC0063/"}],"strategies":[{"id":"DET0764","name":"Detection of Adversary-in-the-Middle","url":"https://attack.mitre.org/detectionstrategies/DET0764","stix_id":"x-mitre-detection-strategy--b6a6d95c-e3b5-438d-a095-3fb0859c8f45","relationship_id":"relationship--321dfef3-01bf-40ee-901e-6354b945c31a"}],"analytics":[{"id":"AN1896","name":"Analytic 1896","url":"https://attack.mitre.org/detectionstrategies/DET0764#AN1896","guidance":"Monitor HKLM\\Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\DNSClient for changes to the \"EnableMulticast\" DWORD value. A value of \"0\" indicates LLMNR is disabled. Host-based implementations of this technique may utilize networking-based system calls or network utility commands (e.g., iptables) to locally intercept traffic. Monitor for relevant process creation events. Monitor for network traffic originating from unknown/unexpected hosts. Local network traffic metadata (such as source MAC addressing) as well as usage of network management protocols such as DHCP may be helpful in identifying hardware. For added context on adversary procedures and background see Adversary-in-the-Middle and applicable…","platforms":["None"],"strategy_id":"DET0764"}],"log_sources":[{"component_id":"DC0063","component":"Windows Registry Key Modification","name":"Windows Registry","channel":"None","platforms":["None"],"analytic_id":"AN1896","source_url":"https://attack.mitre.org/detectionstrategies/DET0764#AN1896","validation":"upstream_definition_not_locally_validated","component_page":"telemetry/DC0063/"},{"component_id":"DC0032","component":"Process Creation","name":"Process","channel":"None","platforms":["None"],"analytic_id":"AN1896","source_url":"https://attack.mitre.org/detectionstrategies/DET0764#AN1896","validation":"upstream_definition_not_locally_validated","component_page":"telemetry/DC00